  body: "# Burden of proof in DMG decisions\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nThe DMG describes the burden of proof
    as depending on the issue: initial claims, exceptions, allegations, revision or supersession applications and
    recoverable overpayments have distinct allocations.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\nDo not infer that the claimant
    always bears the burden. The source also requires assistance in obtaining relevant evidence and contains a prescribed-disease
    qualification. Allocation must be reviewed in its benefit and procedural context.\n\n## Inspected source passages\n\
    \n- [DMG 01405, PDF page 52](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/68e8e657cf65bd04bad76752/dmg-v-1-ch-01.pdf#page=52)
    — DMG Vol 1 Ch 1: Principles of decision making and evidence.\n\n## Evidence and review status\n\nModel-assisted
    interpretation of the 15 September 2026 source snapshot; unreviewed and requiring specialist review. The source
    is DWP guidance, not a consolidated statement of legislation. This is an independent experimental publication,
    not an official DWP document, an entitlement decision or an executable benefits rule.\n\nPDF page numbers are
    one-based file positions. Machine extraction can alter reading order, footnotes and characters. Quoted relationships
    preserve extracted text; no visual or specialist acceptance is claimed. Source dates, legal effects and current
    applicability remain separate review tasks.\n\n- `dmg-vol1-ch1` PDF SHA-256: `58b168c7b0a01f54713f6cfa60953549ebc099cbe568ffaeea551d0b756495d2`;
    extracted pages SHA-256: `cf7a000e2c54c5361a03ec4ad73fa22cc14e8cdb0d4f03b53919a4a509a6eacb`.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/dmg-evidence-assessment
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The source connects identifying who bears the burden with weighing evidence and deciding
      whether to seek more.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol1-ch1/0052
      quote: "01405 A clear understanding of where the burden of proof lies helps the DM to weigh the evidence and\n\
        decide whether further evidence should be sought. DMs should note that\n\n\n1. initially the burden lies
        with the claimant to prove that the conditions for a claim or application are\n\nsatisfied1 but they should
        do as much as possible to ensure that the claimant has every opportunity to\nprovide all relevant evidence
        and where the information is available to them rather than the claimant,\nthen they must make the necessary
        steps to enable it to be traced\n\n\n2. where they wish to show that an exception to a condition of entitlement
        is not satisfied, the burden of\n\nproof rests with them2\n\n\n3. there is no presumption in favour of the
        claimant though for IIDB the claimant is normally presumed to\nhave the PD if he has worked in the prescribed
        occupation; for example, a cotton weaver with byssinosis\n(see DMG Chapter 67 for full guidance)\n\n\n4.
        where an allegation is denied by the claimant it is generally for DMs to prove the facts.\n\n\n5. the burden
        of proving that the conditions for revision or supersession are satisfied lies with the person\nwho applies
        for revision or supersession\n\n\n6. in overpayment cases the burden of proof for the purposes of determining
        the sum to be recovered\n\nfalls on them3 (see DMG Chapter 09)."
      locator: DMG 01405
